[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Dependency Injection and Inversion of Control Containers"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to Inversion of Control (IoC)"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Inversion of Control (IoC) is a design principle in software engineering where the control of object creation, configuration, and lifecycle is shifted away from the application code and handed over to an external container or framework. This decouples components and makes your codebase modular, testable, and easier to maintain."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Dependency Injection (DI)"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"Dependency Injection is a specific implementation technique of IoC. Instead of a class constructing its own dependencies internally, those dependencies are "injected" or passed into the class from the outside. Typically, this is done via constructor parameters, property setters, or interface methods."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Code Example: Without Dependency Injection"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider a tightly coupled class where the Engine is instantiated directly inside the Car class. This makes unit testing difficult and violates the Dependency Inversion Principle."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"class V8Engine {
  public void start() {
    System.out.println("V8 engine started");
  }
}

class Car {
  private V8Engine engine;

  public Car() {
    this.engine = new V8Engine(); // Tightly coupled
  }

  public void drive() {
    engine.start();
  }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Code Example: With Constructor Injection"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"By introducing an interface and passing the dependency through the constructor, we decouple the Car from any specific engine implementation."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"interface Engine {
  void start();
}

class ElectricEngine implements Engine {
  public void start() {
    System.out.println("Electric engine started");
  }
}

class Car {
  private Engine engine;

  public Car(Engine engine) {
    this.engine = engine; // Dependency injected
  }

  public void drive() {
    engine.start();
  }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"The Role of IoC Containers"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"As applications grow, manually instantiating classes and passing dependencies down large object graphs becomes tedious. An IoC Container automates this process by managing object creation, wiring up dependencies, and controlling object lifetimes."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Service Registration: The container is configured to know which implementations map to which abstractions or interfaces."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Resolution: The container automatically inspects constructors and supplies the required instances."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lifecycle Management: The container handles singletons, transient instances, and scoped objects seamlessly."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Dependency Injection and IoC containers form the backbone of modern enterprise application architecture. By utilizing these patterns, developers achieve loosely coupled codebases that facilitate rapid feature addition, mocking in unit tests, and simplified configuration management."}]
